<section id="about">
  <p class="section-label">About</p>
  <p class="section-intro">
    I design and evaluate AI systems with a focus on measurable safety, reliability, and bias reduction.
  </p>
  <div class="focus">
    <p class="focus-label">Current focus</p>
    <p class="focus-text">
      When can we trust an evaluation that says a model
      can't do something?
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose-block">
    <p class="prose">
      I build evaluation frameworks and observability infrastructure for deployed AI systems.
      At <span class="highlight">Arva AI</span>, I redesigned benchmarking pipelines and built
      end-to-end agentic tracing - replacing unstructured logs with structured visibility
      across the full agent lifecycle.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      My research background is in <span class="highlight">clinical AI</span> - specifically
      demographic bias in pediatric mental health records. That work, developed during two years
      as a Research Assistant at Queen Mary University of London, was published in
      <span class="highlight">Nature (2026)</span>.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Most recently I've been testing whether frontier models can <span class="highlight">fake weakness</span>
      on capability evaluations. I borrowed tests that forensic neuropsychologists use to catch
      people faking memory loss, ran them on Gemini, GPT-5-mini and DeepSeek, and found that every
      verdict depended on what you compare the model against. If our tests only catch the models
      that are bad at hiding, passing one tells us less than we think.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p class="closing">
    Open to research roles and fellowships in AI safety, dangerous-capability evaluation, and AI control. Also building toward AI governance, supported by AI Governance coursework from Saïd Business School, University of Oxford.
  </p>
</section>

